#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097831 :tutorial/content "It's often helpful to express a given vector in terms of sums and scalar multiples of a handful of known vectors.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following diagram:\n\n![](images/a6/a6c8dcdf9be32345bbc92ff162992e6c9cf02c107de8ff74caf534b508bfe411.png)\n\nHow can we express the vector $\\overrightarrow{PR}$ in terms of the vectors $\\textbf{a}$ and $\\textbf{b}$ only?\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QR}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c72ba23124e9622b50f9daee4006ca4aac6a460b8a694a368d0e53393a85053.png)\n\nFrom the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{PQ}}}   = \\mathbf{a} \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n{\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{QR}}}  = -\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9d284b2d2683ae43fe57e8c96433803bc908d764e489ac0f861ad978393fde7d.png)\n\nTherefore, by substituting these values into our equation for ${\\color{red}\\overrightarrow{PR}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QR} \\\\  & =\\text{a}+(-\\text{b}) \\\\  & =\\text{a}-\\text{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d92705a2-c9e5-5782-91e1-fa086fa5bffb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2904 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
